\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Gaussian sectors and their retained scalars}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{For the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) with #{t,m>0},
the two real \vocabk{half-turn sectors}{fdip-000L} are independent
under the Gaussian law. Their precision and covariance blocks vary
with #{t,m}, although the action and its projectors do not. Put
#{a=m^2} and #{s=1+t+a}. In vertex order #{(0,1,2,3)}, the
\vocabk{massive precision}{fdip-000A} is
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}
s&-1&0&-t\\-1&s&-t&0\\0&-t&s&-1\\-t&0&-1&s
\end{pmatrix}.}
Use the orthonormal vectors
##{\begin{aligned}
v_{\varepsilon,0}&=(\delta_0+\varepsilon\delta_2)/\sqrt2,\\
v_{\varepsilon,1}&=(\delta_1+\varepsilon\delta_3)/\sqrt2,
\qquad\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}.
\end{aligned}}
Write #{\phi=\sum_\varepsilon
(x_\varepsilon v_{\varepsilon,0}+y_\varepsilon v_{\varepsilon,1})}
and #{z_\varepsilon=(x_\varepsilon,y_\varepsilon)}. These are
normalized sector coordinates, rather than the coefficients in the
unnormalized free bases of \ref{fdip-000L}.}

\p{In the order #{(v_{+,0},v_{+,1},v_{-,0},v_{-,1})}, the
precision is #{\operatorname{diag}(Q_+,Q_-)} and its inverse is
#{\operatorname{diag}(G_+,G_-)}, where
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_+&=\begin{pmatrix}s&-(1+t)\\-(1+t)&s\end{pmatrix},\\
Q_-&=\begin{pmatrix}s&t-1\\t-1&s\end{pmatrix},\\
G_+&=\tfrac1{a(a+2+2t)}
     \begin{pmatrix}s&1+t\\1+t&s\end{pmatrix},\\
G_-&=\tfrac1{(a+2)(a+2t)}
     \begin{pmatrix}s&1-t\\1-t&s\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The \vocabk{covariance}{fdip-000B} in these coordinates is given
by the displayed inverse blocks. If #{T\phi=(z_+,z_-)}, then
##{T_*\mu_Q=\mu_{Q_+}\otimes\mu_{Q_-}.}
The product on the right is the product probability measure.
In particular the sector random vectors #{z_+,z_-} are independent.}

\p{\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}
gives the Gaussian covariance and coordinate-change convention.
The [positive Gaussian normalization](fdip-0005) and
[inverse-precision covariance](fdip-000B) apply to each block.
The product assertion follows from the explicit density calculation
below, rather than from the algebraic direct sum alone.}

\proof{
\p{Each unnormalized vector has squared norm two, and the pairings
between distinct vectors are zero. Thus the basis matrix #{O} with the
displayed columns is orthogonal, #{T=O^T}, and
#{|\det O|=1}. Applying #{Q} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
Qv_{+,0}&=sv_{+,0}-(1+t)v_{+,1},\\
Qv_{+,1}&=-(1+t)v_{+,0}+sv_{+,1},\\
Qv_{-,0}&=sv_{-,0}+(t-1)v_{-,1},\\
Qv_{-,1}&=(t-1)v_{-,0}+sv_{-,1}.
\end{aligned}}
These are precisely the two asserted blocks. Their sum and difference
vectors have eigenvalues
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_+&:\quad a,\ a+2+2t,\\
Q_-&:\quad a+2t,\ a+2.
\end{aligned}}
They are all positive. Multiplication by the displayed #{G_\pm}
gives the identity: the diagonal numerators are respectively
#{s^2-(1+t)^2=a(a+2+2t)} and
#{s^2-(t-1)^2=(a+2)(a+2t)}, and the off-diagonal numerators
vanish. This proves the inverse formulas.}

\p{The orthogonal change of variables preserves coordinate Lebesgue
measure and writes the Euclidean action as
##{\mathcal A(\phi)=\tfrac12 z_+^TQ_+z_+
                         +\tfrac12 z_-^TQ_-z_-.}
Each factor has a finite positive normalizer
##{Z_\pm=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\det Q_\pm}}.}
Tonelli's theorem applied to the nonnegative density gives
#{Z_Q=Z_+Z_-}. Division by this product yields the product of the
two normalized densities. Integrating over every Borel rectangle
#{A_+\times A_-} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\mu_Q\{z_+\in A_+,\ z_-\in A_-\}\\
&\qquad=\mu_{Q_+}(A_+)\mu_{Q_-}(A_-).
\end{aligned}}
This identifies the product law and proves independence. In particular
it does not assume that zero covariance alone implies independence.
Polynomial moments are integrable by \ref{fdip-0006}.}
}

\p{Retain #{R=(0,2)}. Its orthonormal sector vectors are
##{w_\varepsilon=(\delta_0^R+\varepsilon\delta_2^R)/\sqrt2.}
The projection satisfies
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi_Rv_{\varepsilon,0}&=w_\varepsilon,\\
\pi_Rv_{\varepsilon,1}&=0.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the retained sector coordinates are exactly #{x_+,x_-},
with no additional factor of #{\sqrt2}. In these coordinates, the
[retained precision](fdip-000F) has positive scalar blocks
##{\begin{aligned}
\sigma_+&=s-\frac{(1+t)^2}{s}
          =\frac{a(a+2+2t)}s,\\
\sigma_-&=s-\frac{(t-1)^2}{s}
          =\frac{(a+2)(a+2t)}s.
\end{aligned}}
The retained coordinates are independent centered Gaussians with
variances #{\sigma_+^{-1},\sigma_-^{-1}}. Decomposing into
sectors and taking this marginal therefore give the same law in
either order.}

\proof{
\p{A sector has precision
#{\begin{pmatrix}s&c\\c&s\end{pmatrix}}, with
#{c=-(1+t)} or #{c=t-1}. Complete the square:
##{\begin{aligned}
sx^2+2cxy+sy^2
 &=s(y+cx/s)^2\\
 &{}+(s-c^2/s)x^2.
\end{aligned}}
The [scalar Gaussian integral](fdip-0005) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\mathbb R}
   e^{-(sx^2+2cxy+sy^2)/2}\,dy\\
&\qquad=\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{s}}
   e^{-(s-c^2/s)x^2/2}.
\end{aligned}}
Each eliminated sector coordinate contributes
#{\sqrt{2\pi/s}}; the two factors multiply to #{2\pi/s}.
This is exactly the factor for the original eliminated block
#{C=sI_2} in \ref{fdip-000F}. The retained orthogonal change of
coordinates also has absolute determinant one. Normalizing the two
scalar densities proves the claimed retained product law.}

\p{Equivalently, the effective precision in vertex coordinates is
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&-\beta\\-\beta&\alpha\end{pmatrix},\\
\alpha&=s-\frac{1+t^2}{s},\qquad\beta=\frac{2t}{s}.
\end{aligned}}
It acts on #{w_+} by #{\alpha-\beta=\sigma_+}, and on
#{w_-} by #{\alpha+\beta=\sigma_-}. Thus each sector's
\vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D} agrees with the corresponding
block of #{S}.}

\p{See \citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}
for the general block formula.
Restricting #{Q} to a full sector still leaves two coordinates.
Integrating out its second coordinate takes the Schur complement
computed above.}
}

\p{Transforming the retained coordinates back gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}(\phi_0)
 =\operatorname{Var}(\phi_2)
 &=\tfrac12(\sigma_+^{-1}+\sigma_-^{-1}),\\
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q
 &=\tfrac12(\sigma_+^{-1}-\sigma_-^{-1}).
\end{aligned}}
For #{m=t=1}, the sector blocks and their inverses are
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_+&=\begin{pmatrix}3&-2\\-2&3\end{pmatrix},&Q_-&=3I_2,\\
G_+&=\tfrac15\begin{pmatrix}3&2\\2&3\end{pmatrix},&G_-&=I_2/3.
\end{aligned}}
Hence #{\sigma_+=5/3}, #{\sigma_-=3}; the retained variance
is #{7/15} and the cross covariance is #{2/15}. For #{m=1,t=2},
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_+&=\begin{pmatrix}4&-3\\-3&4\end{pmatrix},\\
Q_-&=\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\1&4\end{pmatrix},\\
G_+&=\tfrac17\begin{pmatrix}4&3\\3&4\end{pmatrix},\\
G_-&=\tfrac1{15}\begin{pmatrix}4&-1\\-1&4\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Now #{\sigma_+=7/4}, #{\sigma_-=15/4}; the retained variance
is #{44/105} and the cross covariance is #{16/105}. Both examples
have the same #{P,J,E_\pm,F_\pm}, but different covariance
matrices and hence different Gaussian states.}

\p{The remaining freedom is visible in the algebra of operators
commuting with #{P}. In the sector basis,
#{P=\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2)}. For a block operator
#{M=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{pmatrix}},
##{MP=PM\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad B=C=0,}
because the off-diagonal equations are #{2B=2C=0} over
#{\mathbb R}. Its commutant is therefore
#{M_2(\mathbb R)\times M_2(\mathbb R)}. The image of
#{\mathbb R[C_2]} is only
##{\{\operatorname{diag}(xI_2,yI_2):x,y\in\mathbb R\}.}
Each sign occurs twice in the full module, so symmetry allows mixing
within its two-dimensional sector. It does not require #{Q_\pm}
to be scalar. The [distinction between mathematical summands and
physical interpretations](fgap-0013) applies: this decomposition
alone selects no continuous gauge group, physical mass spectrum or
interaction.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr}
supplies the volume-preserving passage to orthonormal coordinates.
The concrete block matrices, product-density proof and sectorwise
Schur calculation above supply the additional content for this
family. The [covariance providers](fdip-000B) use covariance as
their parameter, so they apply with #{G_\pm=Q_\pm^{-1}}.}
